window.FIELD_CHAPTER_PARTS["book-3-full-book"][123] = "<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>ID</th>\n<th>Type</th>\n<th>Difficulty</th>\n<th>Source chapter</th>\n<th>Competency</th>\n<th>Company tag</th>\n<th>Prompt (short)</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>IQ-C27-07</td>\n<td>strategy-gtm</td>\n<td>Hard</td>\n<td>C27</td>\n<td>ST</td>\n<td>—</td>\n<td>You are the PM of an internal platform used by 40 teams. Only 12 have adopted it. What do you do?</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>IQ-C27-08</td>\n<td>strategy-gtm</td>\n<td>Hard</td>\n<td>C27</td>\n<td>ST</td>\n<td>—</td>\n<td>As a growth PM you raised weekly active users by 12%, but 90-day retention fell. What do you do?</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>IQ-C27-09</td>\n<td>strategy-gtm</td>\n<td>Hard</td>\n<td>C27</td>\n<td>ST</td>\n<td>—</td>\n<td>What is different about building a product in a regulated domain such as health?</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>IQ-C28-01</td>\n<td>product-sense</td>\n<td>Beginner</td>\n<td>C28</td>\n<td>PS</td>\n<td>—</td>\n<td>Pick a product you use every day and critique it in ten minutes.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>IQ-C28-02</td>\n<td>product-sense</td>\n<td>Beginner</td>\n<td>C28</td>\n<td>PS</td>\n<td>—</td>\n<td>In a critique, what is the difference between an observation, an inference and an opinion? Give an example.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>IQ-C28-03</td>\n<td>product-sense</td>\n<td>Intermediate</td>\n<td>C28</td>\n<td>PS</td>\n<td>Apple</td>\n<td>Critique the setup experience of wireless earbuds sold with a phone, and say what you would change.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>IQ-C28-04</td>\n<td>product-sense</td>\n<td>Intermediate</td>\n<td>C28</td>\n<td>PS</td>\n<td>Google</td>\n<td>How would you decide whether to build a travel mode for a specific segment in a new market, such as motorcy...</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>IQ-C28-05</td>\n<td>product-sense</td>\n<td>Intermediate</td>\n<td>C28</td>\n<td>PS</td>\n<td>Netflix</td>\n<td>You are rolling out paid sharing for a subscription service. How do you plan it, and what do you tell leade...</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>IQ-C28-06</td>\n<td>product-sense</td>\n<td>Intermediate</td>\n<td>C28</td>\n<td>PS</td>\n<td>Meta</td>\n<td>A messaging app encrypts every message so the company cannot read it. How do you fight spam and abuse?</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>IQ-C28-07</td>\n<td>product-sense</td>\n<td>Hard</td>\n<td>C28</td>\n<td>PS</td>\n<td>—</td>\n<td>A recommendation feed has high return rates, but you suspect it is narrowing what people see. How do you ch...</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>IQ-C28-08</td>\n<td>product-sense</td>\n<td>Hard</td>\n<td>C28</td>\n<td>PS</td>\n<td>Apple</td>\n<td>A watch notifies a wearer of a possible irregular heart rhythm. Design what happens after the alert.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>IQ-C28-09</td>\n<td>product-sense</td>\n<td>Intermediate</td>\n<td>C28</td>\n<td>PS</td>\n<td>—</td>\n<td>A bold redesign of search, launched as the company's biggest change in a decade, appears to be hurting user...</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>IQ-C29-01</td>\n<td>execution</td>\n<td>Beginner</td>\n<td>C29</td>\n<td>EX</td>\n<td>—</td>\n<td>What is a feature factory, and how would you avoid being one?</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>IQ-C29-02</td>\n<td>execution</td>\n<td>Beginner</td>\n<td>C29</td>\n<td>EX</td>\n<td>—</td>\n<td>What is a vanity metric? Give an example and a better alternative.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>IQ-C29-03</td>\n<td>execution</td>\n<td>Intermediate</td>\n<td>C29</td>\n<td>EX</td>\n<td>—</td>\n<td>A senior stakeholder insists you build a feature because a competitor has it. What do you do?</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>IQ-C29-04</td>\n<td>execution</td>\n<td>Intermediate</td>\n<td>C29</td>\n<td>EX</td>\n<td>—</td>\n<td>Users keep asking for a feature. Do you build it?</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>IQ-C29-05</td>\n<td>execution</td>\n<td>Intermediate</td>\n<td>C29</td>\n<td>EX</td>\n<td>—</td>\n<td>Your launch hit its date, but the metric did not move. What do you do?</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>IQ-C29-06</td>\n<td>behavioral</td>\n<td>Intermediate</td>\n<td>C29</td>\n<td>BE</td>\n<td>—</td>\n<td>How do you spot your own recurring mistakes?</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>IQ-C29-07</td>\n<td>execution</td>\n<td>Hard</td>\n<td>C29</td>\n<td>EX</td>\n<td>—</td>\n<td>A product has 40 million registered users, but most sessions are very short. Is it healthy?</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>IQ-C29-08</td>\n<td>behavioral</td>\n<td>Hard</td>\n<td>C29</td>\n<td>BE</td>\n<td>—</td>\n<td>You discover your team has been optimizing a metric that does not matter for the last six months. What do y...</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>IQ-C30-01</td>\n<td>execution</td>\n<td>Beginner</td>\n<td>C30</td>\n<td>EX</td>\n<td>—</td>\n<td>How would you take a new product idea from discovery to launch?</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>IQ-C30-02</td>\n<td>execution</td>\n<td>Beginner</td>\n<td>C30</td>\n<td>EX</td>\n<td>—</td>\n<td>What is the difference between discovery, alpha, beta and live?</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>IQ-C30-03</td>\n<td>product-sense</td>\n<td>Intermediate</td>\n<td>C30</td>\n<td>PS</td>\n<td>—</td>\n<td>You are building a learning app for students in a market with low-end phones and weak data. What goes into ...</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>IQ-C30-04</td>\n<td>strategy-gtm</td>\n<td>Intermediate</td>\n<td>C30</td>\n<td>ST</td>\n<td>—</td>\n<td>How would you start a marketplace connecting small neighborhood shops with wholesalers?</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>IQ-C30-05</td>\n<td>strategy-gtm</td>\n<td>Intermediate</td>\n<td>C30</td>\n<td>ST</td>\n<td>—</td>\n<td>Your enterprise pilot succeeded at three sites. Why might the deal still stall, and what do you do?</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>IQ-C30-06</td>\n<td>ai</td>\n<td>Hard</td>\n<td>C30</td>\n<td>AI</td>\n<td>—</td>\n<td>An agent drafts purchase orders and a person approves each one. The median approval takes three seconds. Is...</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>IQ-C30-07</td>\n<td>ai</td>\n<td>Hard</td>\n<td>C30</td>\n<td>AI</td>\n<td>—</td>\n<td>You run an internal AI evaluation platform used by 40 product teams. You cannot mandate adoption. How do yo...</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>IQ-C30-08</td>\n<td>behavioral</td>\n<td>Hard</td>\n<td>C30</td>\n<td>BE</td>\n<td>—</td>\n<td>Tell me about a product you took from idea to launch.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>IQ-C31-01</td>\n<td>behavioral</td>\n<td>Beginner</td>\n<td>C31</td>\n<td>BE</td>\n<td>—</td>\n<td>Tell me about yourself.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>IQ-C31-02</td>\n<td>behavioral</td>\n<td>Beginner</td>\n<td>C31</td>\n<td>BE</td>\n<td>—</td>\n<td>Why product management, and why this company and role?</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>IQ-C31-03</td>\n<td>behavioral</td>\n<td>Intermediate</td>\n<td>C31</td>\n<td>BE</td>\n<td>—</td>\n<td>Tell me about a time you disagreed with an engineer about scope.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>IQ-C31-04</td>\n<td>behavioral</td>\n<td>Intermediate</td>\n<td>C31</td>\n<td>BE</td>\n<td>Amazon</td>\n<td>Tell me about a time you disagreed with a decision and committed anyway.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>IQ-C31-05</td>\n<td>behavioral</td>\n<td>Intermediate</td>\n<td>C31</td>\n<td>BE</td>\n<td>Apple</td>\n<td>Tell me about a product detail you obsessed over, and what it cost.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>IQ-C31-06</td>\n<td>behavioral</td>\n<td>Hard</td>\n<td>C31</td>\n<td>BE</td>\n<td>Apple</td>\n<td>Describe a time you protected user privacy against a business request.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>IQ-C31-07</td>\n<td>behavioral</td>\n<td>Intermediate</td>\n<td>C31</td>\n<td>BE</td>\n<td>Google</td>\n<td>Tell me about a time you worked on an ambiguous problem.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>IQ-C31-08</td>\n<td>behavioral</td>\n<td>Intermediate</td>\n<td>C31</td>\n<td>BE</td>\n<td>Meta</td>\n<td>Tell me about a time you moved fast and it went wrong.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>IQ-C31-09</td>\n<td>behavioral</td>\n<td>Hard</td>\n<td>C31</td>\n<td>BE</td>\n<td>Netflix</td>\n<td>Tell me about a time you gave candid feedback to someone more senior than you.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>IQ-C31-10</td>\n<td>behavioral</td>\n<td>Intermediate</td>\n<td>C31</td>\n<td>BE</td>\n<td>Microsoft</td>\n<td>Tell me about a time you changed your mind about a product decision because of new information.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>IQ-C31-11</td>\n<td>behavioral</td>\n<td>Hard</td>\n<td>C31</td>\n<td>BE</td>\n<td>—</td>\n<td>A launch is tomorrow, and you discover a defect that corrupts the export for about 3% of users. What do you...</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>IQ-C31-12</td>\n<td>behavioral</td>\n<td>Intermediate</td>\n<td>C31</td>\n<td>BE</td>\n<td>—</td>\n<td>Tell me about a time you worked with a teammate who was not delivering.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>IQ-C32-01</td>\n<td>behavioral</td>\n<td>Beginner</td>\n<td>C32</td>\n<td>BE</td>\n<td>—</td>\n<td>Summarize a complex product decision for a non-technical executive in sixty seconds.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>IQ-C32-02</td>\n<td>behavioral</td>\n<td>Beginner</td>\n<td>C32</td>\n<td>BE</td>\n<td>—</td>\n<td>How do you structure a weekly update to your manager?</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>IQ-C32-03</td>\n<td>behavioral</td>\n<td>Intermediate</td>\n<td>C32</td>\n<td>BE</td>\n<td>—</td>\n<td>How do you get an engineer to build something they don't want to build?</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>IQ-C32-04</td>\n<td>behavioral</td>\n<td>Intermediate</td>\n<td>C32</td>\n<td>BE</td>\n<td>Amazon</td>\n<td>You need senior leaders to approve a major change. How would you prepare the written proposal, and what goe...</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>IQ-C32-05</td>\n<td>behavioral</td>\n<td>Intermediate</td>\n<td>C32</td>\n<td>BE</td>\n<td>—</td>\n<td>A senior stakeholder insists on a feature you think will hurt users. You've said no twice. What do you do?</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>IQ-C32-06</td>\n<td>behavioral</td>\n<td>Hard</td>\n<td>C32</td>\n<td>BE</td>\n<td>—</td>\n<td>Two senior stakeholders want opposite things from your product, and you can't satisfy both. What do you do?</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>IQ-C32-07</td>\n<td>behavioral</td>\n<td>Hard</td>\n<td>C32</td>\n<td>BE</td>\n<td>—</td>\n<td>You must decide with about 60% of the data you'd like. How do you communicate your recommendation?</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>IQ-C32-08</td>\n<td>behavioral</td>\n<td>Intermediate</td>\n<td>C32</td>\n<td>BE</td>\n<td>—</td>\n<td>You work with a team about twelve hours away in time. How do you run a project?</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>IQ-C32-09</td>\n<td>behavioral</td>\n<td>Intermediate</td>\n<td>C32</td>\n<td>BE</td>\n<td>—</td>\n<td>How do you make sure a message is understood by engineers, designers and legal?</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>IQ-C32-10</td>\n<td>behavioral</td>\n<td>Hard</td>\n<td>C32</td>\n<td>BE</td>\n<td>—</td>\n<td>You disagree with a data scientist's interpretation of an experiment. How do you resolve it?</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>IQ-C32-11</td>\n<td>behavioral</td>\n<td>Hard</td>\n<td>C32</td>\n<td>BE</td>\n<td>Stripe</td>\n<td>Write the message to executives that a key launch will slip by three weeks because another team's dependenc...</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>IQ-C33-01</td>\n<td>interview-craft</td>\n<td>Beginner</td>\n<td>C33</td>\n<td>IC</td>\n<td>Apple</td>\n<td>Why Apple, and why this team?</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>IQ-C33-02</td>\n<td>interview-craft</td>\n<td>Beginner</td>\n<td>C33</td>\n<td>IC</td>\n<td>—</td>\n<td>How do you research a company before an interview?</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>IQ-C33-03</td>\n<td>interview-craft</td>\n<td>Intermediate</td>\n<td>C33</td>\n<td>IC</td>\n<td>Apple</td>\n<td>A team wants to add a new capability. How do you decide whether it belongs in the operating system or in a ...</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>IQ-C33-04</td>\n<td>interview-craft</td>\n<td>Intermediate</td>\n<td>C33</td>\n<td>IC</td>\n<td>Apple</td>\n<td>Design a feature that works better across a person's devices without penalizing people who own only one.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>IQ-C33-05</td>\n<td>interview-craft</td>\n<td>Hard</td>\n<td>C33</td>\n<td>IC</td>\n<td>Apple</td>\n<td>A team wants to log more usage data to improve a feature. How do you decide?</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>IQ-C33-06</td>\n<td>interview-craft</td>\n<td>Intermediate</td>\n<td>C33</td>\n<td>IC</td>\n<td>Apple</td>\n<td>How do you decide what to leave out of a new feature?</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>IQ-C33-07</td>\n<td>interview-craft</td>\n<td>Hard</td>\n<td>C33</td>\n<td>IC</td>\n<td>Apple</td>\n<td>How would you prioritize accessibility features against features for the mainstream?</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>IQ-C33-08</td>\n<td>interview-craft</td>\n<td>Intermediate</td>\n<td>C33</td>\n<td>IC</td>\n<td>Apple</td>\n<td>How would you improve how families share and organize photos together?</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>IQ-C33-09</td>\n<td>interview-craft</td>\n<td>Intermediate</td>\n<td>C33</td>\n<td>IC</td>\n<td>—</td>\n<td>What questions would you ask your interviewers?</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>IQ-C33-10</td>\n<td>interview-craft</td>\n<td>Intermediate</td>\n<td>C33</td>\n<td>IC</td>\n<td>—</td>\n<td>You receive a 48-hour take-home product case. What do you do?</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>IQ-C33-11</td>\n<td>interview-craft</td>\n<td>Hard</td>\n<td>C33</td>\n<td>IC</td>\n<td>—</td>\n<td>You have loops at two companies in the same month, one built on stories and a written exercise, the other o...</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>IQ-C34-01</td>\n<td>interview-craft</td>\n<td>Beginner</td>\n<td>C34</td>\n<td>IC</td>\n<td>—</td>\n<td>How would you structure your practice for the month before a loop?</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>IQ-C34-02</td>\n<td>interview-craft</td>\n<td>Beginner</td>\n<td>C34</td>\n<td>IC</td>\n<td>—</td>\n<td>How do you give useful feedback to a peer after a mock interview?</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>IQ-C34-03</td>\n<td>interview-craft</td>\n<td>Intermediate</td>\n<td>C34</td>\n<td>IC</td>\n<td>—</td>\n<td>Tell me about a time you received critical feedback.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>IQ-C34-04</td>\n<td>interview-craft</td>\n<td>Intermediate</td>\n<td>C34</td>\n<td>IC</td>\n<td>—</td>\n<td>You did well in mock interviews but froze in the real one. What happened, and what would you change?</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>IQ-C34-05</td>\n<td>interview-craft</td>\n<td>Intermediate</td>\n<td>C34</td>\n<td>IC</td>\n<td>—</td>\n<td>How do you know when you are ready for a loop?</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>IQ-C34-06</td>\n<td>interview-craft</td>\n<td>Hard</td>\n<td>C34</td>\n<td>IC</td>\n<td>—</td>\n<td>How would you use an AI tool to prepare for interviews without fooling yourself?</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>IQ-C34-07</td>\n<td>interview-craft</td>\n<td>Intermediate</td>\n<td>C34</td>\n<td>IC</td>\n<td>—</td>\n<td>Your self-scores are consistently higher than your partner's. What do you do?</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>IQ-C34-08</td>\n<td>interview-craft</td>\n<td>Hard</td>\n<td>C34</td>\n<td>IC</td>\n<td>—</td>\n<td>Design a mock-interview program for a cohort of 30 aspiring product managers.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>IQ-C34-09</td>\n<td>interview-craft</td>\n<td>Intermediate</td>\n<td>C34</td>\n<td>IC</td>\n<td>—</td>\n<td>Walk me through the final week before a loop.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>IQ-C34-10</td>\n<td>interview-craft</td>\n<td>Hard</td>\n<td>C34</td>\n<td>IC</td>\n<td>—</td>\n<td>How would you evaluate an AI tool that scores mock interviews before recommending it to others?</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>IQ-C37-01</td>\n<td>interview-craft</td>\n<td>Beginner</td>\n<td>C37</td>\n<td>IC</td>\n<td>—</td>\n<td>How do you keep up with this industry?</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>IQ-C37-02</td>\n<td>interview-craft</td>\n<td>Beginner</td>\n<td>C37</td>\n<td>IC</td>\n<td>—</td>\n<td>What would you do in your first 30 days as an associate product manager?</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>IQ-C37-03</td>\n<td>interview-craft</td>\n<td>Intermediate</td>\n<td>C37</td>\n<td>IC</td>\n<td>—</td>\n<td>How did you prepare for this interview, and what did you learn about yourself?</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>IQ-C37-04</td>\n<td>behavioral</td>\n<td>Intermediate</td>\n<td>C37</td>\n<td>BE</td>\n<td>—</td>\n<td>Tell me about a time you taught yourself something quickly.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>IQ-C37-05</td>\n<td>interview-craft</td>\n<td>Intermediate</td>\n<td>C37</td>\n<td>IC</td>\n<td>—</td>\n<td>There is far too much to learn for this role. How do you decide what to learn first?</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>IQ-C37-06</td>\n<td>execution</td>\n<td>Intermediate</td>\n<td>C37</td>\n<td>EX</td>\n<td>—</td>\n<td>What would you ship in your first 90 days, and how would you know it worked?</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>IQ-C37-07</td>\n<td>behavioral</td>\n<td>Hard</td>\n<td>C37</td>\n<td>BE</td>\n<td>—</td>\n<td>Three weeks into the job your manager is very busy and gives you no direction. What do you do?</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>IQ-C37-08</td>\n<td>behavioral</td>\n<td>Hard</td>\n<td>C37</td>\n<td>BE</td>\n<td>—</td>\n<td>After 90 days, how will you know whether you are performing at the level expected of an associate product m...</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<span id=\"app-e-full-entries\"></span>\n<h2>Full entries</h2>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c01-01\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C01-01</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> interview-craft · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Beginner · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C01 · <strong>Competency:</strong> IC · <strong>Company tag:</strong> —</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> What does a product manager do day to day, and where do you have authority?</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;They manage the product, set the roadmap and work with engineers.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Names decisions, partners and the difference between owning an outcome and owning people.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Describes a concrete week with the shares of time, states decision rights clearly, and says what the role is not.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> &quot;A PM owns the outcome of a product or a feature: deciding what is worth building and why, defining how success is measured, and making the launch call, and getting it done with engineering, design and data, who report elsewhere. So my authority is over the <em>what</em> and <em>why</em> and the priorities I propose to my manager; engineering decides how it is built and how long it takes, design decides the experience, data science decides what the data supports. That is why influence matters: I bring the user problem, the evidence and the tradeoff, and make it easy for others to decide well. &quot;Day to day, in a typical week on a feature team: about two thirds of my time is communication, which is writing (a spec, an update, a decision document), working sessions with engineering and design, and aligning stakeholders; about a fifth is data and analysis; the rest is users and planning. I do not build the product. For example, last quarter I found that 38% of users dropped at one step, wrote a one-page proposal, agreed the scope with the engineering lead and design, and we raised completion six points.&quot; <em>Signals.</em> Decisions and partners; authority stated accurately; a concrete example.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c01-02\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C01-02</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> interview-craft · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Beginner · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C01 · <strong>Competency:</strong> IC · <strong>Company tag:</strong> —</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> How is a product manager different from a program manager, a product owner and a technical product manager?</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;They're all roles that work with engineers.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Separates the core responsibility of each.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Distinguishes outcomes from delivery and process, notes that titles vary by company, and says what to check in a posting.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> &quot;I'd separate them by what each is responsible for. A product manager decides what is worth building and why, and owns product outcomes: adoption, retention, revenue. A program manager makes sure what has been decided is delivered across teams: schedules, dependencies, risks. A product owner is a role from the Scrum method: someone who maintains the team's backlog and accepts work; at some companies it is a PM, at others a narrower job. A technical product manager is a PM for a technical product, such as an API or infrastructure, and needs more technical depth. &quot;They overlap, and titles vary between companies, so I'd read the duties in a posting, not the title. If the duties are scheduling and tickets, it is a program or product-owner role. If the duties include choosing what to build, defining metrics and owning an outcome, it is product management whatever it is called. I'd want to work in the second, which is why I'm applying.&quot; <em>Signals.</em> Clear core responsibilities; awareness that titles vary; a practical test.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c01-03\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C01-03</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> interview-craft · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Intermediate · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C01 · <strong>Competency:</strong> IC · <strong>Company tag:</strong> —</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> Who are your most important partners as a PM, and what does each need from you?</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;Engineers and designers; I'd keep them informed.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Names the partners and what each needs.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Includes data science, legal, support and the manager; gives specific needs; gives an example of changing how the candidate worked for a partner.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> &quot;Engineering, design, data science, and my manager first; then legal and privacy, support, product marketing and program management. Engineers need a clear problem, stable scope, honest priorities and time to do quality work; they dislike surprises. Designers need the user problem and evidence, not a solution, and room to explore. Data science needs a precise question and agreed metrics before the analysis, and honest use of results. My manager needs the answer first and bad news early. Legal and privacy need early engagement with the facts; support needs advance notice of changes. &quot;An example: an engineer once told me that my last-minute changes to scope were costing him a day each time. I started freezing scope at the start of each sprint and sending changes as a written request with the tradeoff; changes fell from about four a sprint to one, and he said he could plan again. The lesson was that what a partner needs is often a predictable process, not a bigger effort from me.&quot; <em>Signals.</em> Specific needs; breadth; a changed behavior with a number.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c01-04\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C01-04</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> interview-craft · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Intermediate · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C01 · <strong>Competency:</strong> IC · <strong>Company tag:</strong> —</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> You have about two years of experience. Why an associate role?</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;I want to learn, and I'm early in my career.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Describes the scope owned so far and the gap the role fills.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> States level fit honestly, with scope and evidence, what the associate level would let the candidate learn, why this route, and awareness of eligibility.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> &quot;I'm applying for an associate role because it matches the scope I've owned and the gap I want to close. In my current role I own the metrics for onboarding and have run two experiments and one launch, which raised completion by six points. What I haven't done is carry a roadmap through a quarter of competing priorities, decide between several teams' requests or lead a launch across functions; the associate level is where I'd do that with guidance, on a scope where I can deliver. I've read the eligibility on each posting and applied to roles that match my experience: direct associate and first-level PM roles and, where the wording allows, early-career programs. I'd rather join at the level where I'll be effective and learn than at a level where I would be stretched past my evidence.&quot; <em>Signals.</em> Honest scope; a defined gap; eligibility awareness.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c01-05\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C01-05</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> interview-craft · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Intermediate · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C01 · <strong>Competency:</strong> IC · <strong>Company tag:</strong> —</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> What would you do in your first week as an APM?</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;I'd meet the team and learn the product.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Names what to learn: users, metrics, partners, decisions.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> A concrete first-week plan: map decision rights, read the metrics and past decisions, meet each partner with specific questions, use the product as a user, find a small early contribution, and agree expectations with the manager.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> &quot;I'd aim to learn how decisions get made and what the numbers say, not to change anything. Day one: meet my manager and agree what success looks like in thirty, sixty and ninety days. Day two: use the product as a user for a few hours, read support tickets and the last quarter's roadmap and launch reviews. Day three: meet each partner, engineering, design, data, support and legal, with the same questions: what do you need from a PM, what has gone well and badly, and what should I know that isn't written down? Day four: look at the product's metrics, how they are defined and what moved last quarter. Day five: write a one-page summary of what I've learned, with questions, and share it with my manager; and I'd pick one small thing to own, such as a metric definition or a recurring report. &quot;I'd also map the team's decision rights early: who decides what, so I'm not surprised later. I wouldn't propose big changes in week one, since I'd lack the context to be right.&quot; <em>Signals.</em> A concrete plan; listening first; decision rights; humility.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c01-06\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C01-06</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> interview-craft · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Intermediate · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C01 · <strong>Competency:</strong> IC · <strong>Company tag:</strong> —</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> What is a common misconception about being a PM?</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;That PMs are in charge.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Names a misconception and corrects it with a reason.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Gives two or three misconceptions with evidence from the candidate's own work, and shows what the reality implies for how to prepare.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> &quot;Three. First, that a PM is in charge: a PM owns an outcome, not people, and everything depends on influence. In my work I never told an engineer what to build; I brought the problem and the evidence, and the plan came from the conversation. Second, that the job is generating ideas: ideas are cheap; the work is choosing, measuring and saying no. When I had four requests and one engineer, the value was in the written tradeoff and the no. Third, that you need to code: you need to understand how things work well enough to ask good questions and judge tradeoffs, which I learned by asking engineers to explain our system and by reading our architecture notes, but I don't write production code. The implication for preparing is to practice decisions, evidence and communication more than frameworks or brainstorming, which is how I've spent my time.&quot; <em>Signals.</em> Specific corrections tied to experience; implications for practice.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c01-07\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C01-07</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> interview-craft · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Hard · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C01 · <strong>Competency:</strong> IC · <strong>Company tag:</strong> —</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> A rotational program offer and a direct first-level PM offer arrive in the same week. How do you decide?</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;I'd take the more prestigious one.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Lists criteria such as learning, scope and pay.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Defines criteria and weights, gathers information from both (team, manager, scope, rotations, conversion), compares with a simple scored table, considers reversibility and risk, and decides with a stated reversal condition.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> &quot;I'd decide with criteria, and get information first. Criteria that matter to me: the scope and ownership I would have in the first year; the quality of the manager and team; learning rate (breadth against depth); the path after the program or role (conversion to a PM role, mobility); the people I'd work with; location and work authorization; and compensation. I'd ask each company concrete questions: in the program, how team matching works, how rotations are chosen and what share convert to PM roles and where; in the direct role, what I'd own, who I'd report to and how performance is judged. &quot;Then a simple scored comparison. Suppose I weight scope 25%, manager and team 25%, learning rate 20%, path 15%, compensation 10% and location 5%, and score each one to five: the program might score scope 3, manager 3 (unknown until matching), learning 5, path 4, compensation 4, location 3; the direct role scope 5, manager 4, learning 3, path 3, compensation 4, location 4. The weighted totals are 3.65 and 3.90 (invented), close enough that the uncertain items (the program's team matching, the direct role's growth path) decide it. I'd resolve those with calls to current employees. I'd note reversibility: a direct role can be left later; a program's value is partly the cohort and the early rotations. The decision I'd make, and what would change it: if the program could tell me my first team and it was strong, I'd lean toward the program; if not, the direct role with a clear scope.&quot; <em>Signals.</em> Criteria and weights; information gathering; a scored table with uncertainty; reversal conditions.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c01-08\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C01-08</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> interview-craft · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Hard · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C01 · <strong>Competency:</strong> IC · <strong>Company tag:</strong> —</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> Three months into an associate PM job, you realize the work is mostly scheduling and tracking, not product decisions. What do you do?</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;I'd quit,&quot; or &quot;I'd wait and see.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Checks the facts and talks to the manager.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Separates a temporary phase from a structural mismatch, gathers evidence on decisions and ownership, proposes a specific change in scope, uses decision rights to define the gap, sets a time-boxed plan, and considers other options if it does not change.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> &quot;First I'd check whether I'm right. I'd write down, for the last month, which decisions I made, which I influenced and which I only recorded; if the share of decisions about what to build and why is near zero, the mismatch is real. I'd also ask whether it is a phase (a launch crunch needing coordination) or the design of the role. &quot;Then I'd talk to my manager with the evidence, not a complaint: 'Here is what I've spent my time on, and here is where I think I can add more: I'd like to own the metric for onboarding and propose the next two experiments.' I'd map the team's decision rights with them to find a decision I can own. I'd propose a specific, small change in scope with a date to review, for example taking the metric and the roadmap input for one surface within the next quarter, and I'd keep delivering the coordination work well, because that earns trust. &quot;If after a quarter nothing changes, I'd look for another route: a different team in the same company (an internal transfer), or another company's direct associate role, using what I've learned about the role. I'd decide on the evidence and the date, not on frustration.&quot; <em>Signals.</em> Evidence before complaint; a specific proposal; a time-boxed plan; options.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c02-01\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C02-01</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> product thinking · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Beginner · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C02 · <strong>Competency:</strong> PS · <strong>Company tag:</strong> —</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> Users keep asking for a dark mode in your app. Would you build it?</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;Yes, users are asking for it, and many apps have it.&quot; No segment, no cost, no measure.</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Asks who is asking and why, estimates demand from available data, names the cost, compares with alternatives and decides with a criterion.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Separates a preference from a need, finds the smallest reversible step, defines a metric and a counter-metric, and says what would change the answer.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> I would start by restating the need behind the request. People who ask for dark mode are usually doing one of three things: reading at night and wanting less glare, saving battery on some displays, or needing higher contrast for accessibility. Those are different problems, and the third is a need rather than a preference. Next I would check the evidence we already have. How many of the requests mention night use, and from which segment? What share of sessions happen between 9pm and 6am? Request counts also overstate demand, because the people who ask are the people who care most, so I would pair them with usage data rather than rely on the count alone. Options of different kinds: follow the phone's system theme setting, which is the smallest option and covers a large share of the need; build an in-app toggle with its own theme; do nothing and invest the effort elsewhere. The system-theme option costs the least and is easy to reverse. A full custom theme costs more design and testing time, and it takes engineering effort away from the work that is competing for it. My recommendation is to follow the system setting first, because it serves most of the need at a fraction of the cost, and to expand to a toggle only if users with the system setting turned on still ask for more control. I would measure how many users have the new theme active and whether night-time session length changes for them, with crash rate and support contacts as guardrails, and the share of users who switch the new theme off within a week as the counter-metric. I would change my mind if the requests cluster in an accessibility segment: contrast then becomes a quality requirement rather than a nice-to-have.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c02-02\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C02-02</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> prioritization · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Beginner · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C02 · <strong>Competency:</strong> EX · <strong>Company tag:</strong> —</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> Sales asks you to build a custom report for one large customer who is threatening to leave. Do you build it?</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;Yes, the customer is important&quot; or &quot;No, we never build one-offs,&quot; with no reasoning.</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Sizes the customer's value, checks whether the need is shared, scopes the smallest version, names the cost to the roadmap, and decides by a stated criterion.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Separates the request from the underlying job, tests whether a configurable version serves others, time-boxes the work, names the precedent cost, and says what would change the answer.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> I would not answer yes or no yet. I would ask what the customer is trying to do with the report, because &quot;a custom report&quot; is one way to meet a need, and the need may be shared by other customers. Then I would size three things. First, the customer's value: say the account is 6% of annual revenue and renews in three months (invented). Second, how many other customers have asked for something similar, from support and sales notes. Third, the cost: two engineers for three weeks (invented), which is work that would otherwise go to the roadmap. My options differ in kind. Build exactly what was asked, gaining the account and giving up three weeks of roadmap plus a precedent that large customers get one-offs. Build a configurable export or report builder that covers this case and others, which costs more now and serves more customers. Offer an interim workaround, such as a data export and a template the customer's analyst can use this week, which is cheap and reversible. I recommend the workaround this week, to remove the immediate threat, and a time-boxed scoping of the configurable version, with the decision based on how many other customers show the same need. The cost is that the customer gets less than they asked for at first, and I would explain why and when they get more. I would change the recommendation if the contract names the report, if the account is large enough that losing it changes the company's plans, or if scoping shows the configurable version costs about the same as the one-off.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c02-03\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C02-03</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> execution · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Intermediate · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C02 · <strong>Competency:</strong> EX · <strong>Company tag:</strong> —</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> Engineering proposes removing a feature that is rarely used but that a few large enterprise customers rely on. How do you decide?</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> Counts users and removes it because &quot;usage is low,&quot; or keeps it because &quot;customers might leave.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Measures revenue and strategic weight of the affected customers, estimates the carrying cost, considers alternatives, and plans the removal.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Classifies the decision as one-way for the customers, reduces the irreversibility with a notice period and a migration path, and states the conditions that would make keeping it right.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> Usage counts are the wrong first measure: a feature used by 2% of accounts can be used by 20% of revenue. So I would ask four questions. Which customers use it, and what share of revenue and renewals do they represent (invented example: 5 accounts, 9% of annual revenue)? What does it cost to keep, in engineering time, incident risk and the drag it adds to other changes? What do the customers do with it, and is there an alternative path? And what is the decision's reversibility? For the customers, removal is a one-way door. They have built workflows on it. That points to the mode &quot;decide with evidence and reduce the irreversibility,&quot; not &quot;decide quickly.&quot; My options: keep it as is; keep it but freeze it, with no new investment; replace it with a supported alternative and migrate customers; remove it with a long notice period. Keeping it as is gives up engineering capacity. Removal gives up the revenue risk and the trust of the customers, whose renewals may be tied to the feature. Replacing it asks us to build something, and costs the most effort but is the only option that serves both sides. I would recommend freezing it now, investigating a replacement path with the five customers, and deciding on removal when we know the replacement cost and each customer's migration effort. If the carrying cost turns out to be large and the replacement path is cheap, I would remove it with at least a quarter's notice and a named migration contact. I would change my mind if the customers' contracts name the feature or if the replacement cost exceeds the revenue at risk.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c02-04\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C02-04</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> product thinking · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Intermediate · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C02 · <strong>Competency:</strong> PR · <strong>Company tag:</strong> —</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> Customer support costs have doubled in a year, while the customer base grew 30%. What do you do?</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;Add a chatbot&quot; or &quot;hire more efficient agents,&quot; with no diagnosis.</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Decomposes cost into volume and cost per contact, finds the drivers, and addresses the largest.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Separates fixed facts from conventions, computes the per-customer change, finds root causes upstream of support, and chooses between deflecting contacts and removing the reason for them.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> I would start with arithmetic, because &quot;doubled&quot; and &quot;grew 30%&quot; are not the same thing. If total cost doubled and customers grew 1.3 times, cost per customer rose by about 54% (2 divided by 1.3, which is 1.54). So growth explains part of the increase, but not most of it. Decompose cost. Cost equals contacts times cost per contact. Contacts equal customers times contact rate. Cost per contact equals handling time times labor cost per minute plus tooling. I would find which factor moved: did the contact rate rise, did handling time lengthen, or did the labor cost change? Suppose the data (invented) show the contact rate per customer up 40%, handling time up 10%, and the rest from labor. The contact rate is the largest driver, so I would examine its reasons. Break contacts by reason and by customer cohort: billing questions, delivery issues, product defects, &quot;how do I&quot; questions. Say billing questions are 35% of the increase (invented). The first-principles question is: what is the user trying to do, and why can they not do it without help? If the billing page is confusing, the fix is a clearer page, which removes contacts rather than handling them. The options differ in kind. A chatbot deflects contacts at the support layer, is quick to build, and leaves the root cause. Improving the billing page removes contacts at the source, takes longer, and benefits customers who never contacted support. Training agents reduces handling time and affects the smaller factor. I would recommend working on the top two contact reasons at the source, with a limited deflection tool for the long tail. I would measure contacts per customer by reason, not total cost, with customer satisfaction as the <a href=\"#gl-guardrail-metric\">guardrail metric</a> so that cost cuts do not come from worse service. I would change course if the top reasons turn out to be outside our control, such as carrier delays.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c02-05\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C02-05</h3>\n";
